# Morchadi Gems — environment variables
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values. .env.local is gitignored and must
# never be committed. In production these are set in Coolify, which deploys the Dockerfile at
# the repo root onto the owner's VPS - see DEPLOY.md for the procedure and ADR-032 for why.
#
# Only variables prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_ are exposed to the browser bundle. Everything
# else is server-only and must be read exclusively inside route handlers or server modules.
#
# BUILD-TIME vs RUNTIME. Coolify keeps these in two separate columns and the distinction is
# not cosmetic - a value in the wrong one fails silently.
#
#   Build-time   NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_WEB3FORMS_KEY,
#                NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID
#                Next inlines every NEXT_PUBLIC_* into the bundle at compile time. Setting
#                one at runtime does nothing at all.
#
#   Runtime      CASHFREE_APP_ID, CASHFREE_SECRET_KEY, CASHFREE_ENV,
#                CALLMEBOT_PHONE, CALLMEBOT_APIKEY, DATABASE_URL,
#                ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_PASSWORD
#                Read through process.env per request in server code. Never pass a secret as
#                a Docker build ARG - ARG values stay readable in the image history.
#                DATABASE_URL is local-development-only today and is not set in Coolify at
#                all - see its own note at the foot of this file and ADR-040.
#
#   Both         APP_BASE_URL, ADMIN_HOSTNAME. See their own notes below.
#
# Nothing here reaches the image by file: .dockerignore excludes .env* from the build context
# entirely, so every value is supplied by Coolify at build or at run.

# Cashfree merchant app ID, from the Cashfree dashboard under Developers > API Keys.
# Server-only. Sandbox and production have different IDs — this must match CASHFREE_ENV.
CASHFREE_APP_ID=your_cashfree_app_id_here

# Cashfree secret key, issued alongside the app ID. Used to sign API requests and to verify
# webhook signatures. Server-only, and the single most sensitive value in this project:
# never import it into a "use client" file, never log it, never send it to the browser.
# Rotate it in the Cashfree dashboard if it is ever exposed.
CASHFREE_SECRET_KEY=your_cashfree_secret_key_here

# Which Cashfree environment to talk to. Selects the API base URL.
#   sandbox    — test credentials, test cards, no real money moves
#   production — live credentials, real charges
# Keep this as sandbox everywhere except the production Vercel environment.
CASHFREE_ENV=sandbox

# Absolute base URL of this deployment, with scheme and no trailing slash. Two things read it,
# and both need it to be the real public origin:
#
#   1. The Cashfree return_url, which must be an origin the shopper's browser can actually
#      reach. Behind a proxy the request's own host header can be an internal name.
#   2. Every absolute URL the site publishes: canonical tags, share cards, /sitemap.xml,
#      /robots.txt and every schema.org @id in the JSON-LD (lib/site-url.ts, ADR-029).
#
# Server-only, and preferred over NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL when both are set.
#
# Set this in BOTH the build and the runtime environment, to the same value. The sitemap,
# robots.txt, canonical tags and every schema.org @id are prerendered, so a value missing at
# build time bakes localhost into the deployed HTML - and a restart will not fix it, only a
# rebuild will. A value missing at runtime sends the Cashfree return_url back to the request
# origin, which behind a proxy can be an internal hostname.
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# The same absolute base URL, exposed to the browser for metadataBase and canonical URLs.
# Public by design — it contains no secret. Also the fallback for the Cashfree return URL
# when APP_BASE_URL is unset; if neither is set, the request's own origin is used.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# CallMeBot WhatsApp notification for the shop owner, from https://www.callmebot.com/blog/free-api-whatsapp-messages/
#
# On a payment the server has verified as PAID, /api/notify-admin sends the owner a WhatsApp
# holding the whole order: items, the letter or colour chosen on each, the delivery address and
# the amount Cashfree charged. There is no database, so that message and the Cashfree dashboard
# are together the order record - it has to carry everything needed to pack the parcel.
#
# Both are server-only and neither is NEXT_PUBLIC_: the key must never reach the browser.
#
# Optional. With either one unset the notification is skipped silently and checkout is
# completely unaffected - a deployment without these sells exactly as well as one with them.
# CallMeBot is a free hobby service and is treated as best-effort throughout: a 5 second
# timeout, no retry, and any failure is logged and swallowed. See docs/api/notify-admin.md.

# The owner's own WhatsApp number: country code first, digits only, no + and no spaces.
CALLMEBOT_PHONE=919358358834

# The key CallMeBot issues to that number after you message their bot to activate it.
CALLMEBOT_APIKEY=your_callmebot_apikey_here

# Google Analytics 4 measurement ID, from the GA4 admin under Data Streams > Web > your stream
# (it looks like G-XXXXXXXXXX). Public by design: a measurement id is visible in the page
# source of every site that uses one, which is why it carries the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# BUILD-TIME. Next inlines it at compile time, so setting it only at runtime does nothing.
#
# Optional. Leave it empty and <GoogleAnalytics /> renders nothing at all - no script tag, no
# request to Google, no dataLayer - and the site behaves exactly as it does today. Campaign
# attribution does not depend on it either: lib/utm.ts records utm_* parameters in the
# browser's localStorage whether or not this is set.
#
# The Content-Security-Policy already allows the two Google hosts the tag needs
# (config/security-headers.mjs), so filling this in is the only step. See ADR-039.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=

# Web3Forms public access key for the /contact form, from https://web3forms.com.
# Public by design: it is a submission token that identifies the destination inbox, not a
# secret, which is why it is safe with the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# Leave it unset and the contact form still validates, but it tells the visitor that message
# delivery is not connected and points them at the support email and WhatsApp instead — it
# never claims to have sent something it did not send. See ADR-012.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WEB3FORMS_KEY=

# Postgres connection string for the Phase 2 order and CRM domain (ADR-040).
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY at this point. The value below matches the throwaway Postgres in
# docker-compose.yml exactly; start it with `docker compose up -d` and this connects. Those
# credentials are deliberately committed — the database is empty, listens on your machine only,
# and holds nothing worth protecting. See docs/DEV-DATABASE.md.
#
# The admin panel is the first thing that genuinely needs it. Admin *credentials* are
# ADMIN_USERNAME/ADMIN_PASSWORD below, not a row in this database (ADR-061) — but a signed-in
# session still is: AdminSession rows are what makes "sign out" actually revoke access rather
# than merely clearing a cookie. Without a database the panel cannot keep anyone signed in.
# Nothing on the storefront reads it — checkout still runs entirely without a database.
#
# RUNTIME, server-only. It is a credential and must never carry the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# The Prisma CLI (prisma generate, prisma migrate, prisma studio) reads .env, NOT .env.local.
# For local work the same value therefore lives in both files — see docs/DEV-DATABASE.md.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://morchadi_dev:dev_local_only@localhost:5432/morchadi_gems_dev

# The admin panel's sole credentials (ADR-061). ADMIN_USERNAME is matched case-insensitively and
# trimmed; ADMIN_PASSWORD is compared byte-for-byte in constant time. Both are checked in
# lib/admin-auth.ts and neither is ever read from, or written to, Postgres.
#
# This trades a bcrypt hash stored in Postgres for a plaintext value sitting in Coolify's env
# panel and the container's process environment — a weaker security posture, accepted because
# resetting a database-backed password required a working SSH tunnel to production, which proved
# unreliable enough to make simple recovery painful.
#
# RUNTIME, server-only, and must be set in both .env.local (local development) and Coolify's
# runtime environment (production) — an unset or empty value makes every login attempt fail
# closed rather than succeed or crash. Rotating either one requires no application code change
# and no database write: edit the value in Coolify and restart or redeploy. There is no in-app
# way to change these — see scripts/seed-admin.mjs, now dead code, for the database-backed
# mechanism this replaced.
ADMIN_USERNAME=your_admin_username_here
ADMIN_PASSWORD=your_admin_password_here

# The hostname the admin panel answers on in production. Optional: with it unset the code
# falls back to admin.morchadigems.com (lib/admin-routing.ts, ADR-041).
#
# It exists so the domain is a deployment's property rather than something written into the
# repository, the same principle APP_BASE_URL follows above. Set it only if the admin panel
# ever moves to a different hostname.
#
# BUILD-TIME and RUNTIME, like APP_BASE_URL. Middleware reads it per request, so runtime is
# what actually matters; set it in both columns anyway so the two never disagree.
#
# NOT NEEDED LOCALLY, and not needed in production until the subdomain exists. There is no
# admin subdomain on a development machine, so /admin/* is served by path instead —
# http://localhost:3000/admin/login — and this variable is not consulted on localhost. In
# production the subdomain does not resolve yet: the Cloudflare DNS record and the Coolify
# domain entry are a later, deployment-focused prompt (ADR-041, "Pending deployment").
ADMIN_HOSTNAME=admin.morchadigems.com
